C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about healing and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes the Scriptures state about healing, it sets the passages in the order they stand, and it answers the strongest objections from named modern writers, so that a reader who disagrees knows where the disagreement lies."),
("p", "The question is narrow. Healing is recorded across the whole Bible, from the LORD who says “I am the LORD that healeth thee” (Exodus 15:26) to the sick of Malta who “came, and were healed” (Acts 28:9). Two things are asked of the passages that bear on the Body of Christ. Does any passage addressed to the Body give healing as a gift, as an authority or as a promise? And what do the letters addressed to the Body actually do with sickness when they meet it?"),
("p", "The answers are taken from the passages. The Lord sent the Twelve to “heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead,” and sent them to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 10:6, 8). Paul lists “the gifts of healing” among the manifestations of the Spirit at Corinth (1 Corinthians 12:9). James writes to “the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad” and tells the sick to call for the elders (James 1:1; 5:14). And the letters addressed to the Body say of their own sick that “God had mercy on him” (Philippians 2:27), that a man should “use a little wine for thy stomach’s sake” (1 Timothy 5:23), and that “Trophimus have I left at Miletum sick” (2 Timothy 4:20)."),
("p", "The position is this. Healing in Scripture is given to a hearer for a purpose the passage names. In the Gospels and in Acts 1 to 8 it confirms a messenger to Israel, and James 5:14 to 16 is Israel’s passage. The gifts of healing at Corinth belong to the sign-program of the overlap, and with the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28 that program ends. Healing ends programmatically at Acts 28. No verse states it; the program ends, and the gift ends with it. In the letters addressed to the Body, sickness is met with prayer, with ordinary care and with God’s mercy, and no letter gives the Body a healing ministry, an authority to heal or a promise of healing."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passages, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where a modern writer is named, the volume says what that writer holds, in its own words, and gives the writer’s words only where it has read them in a free full text."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. The purpose of signs, their audience and the last sign recorded in Acts belong to Volume 1 of this series, and tongues and prophecy to Volume 2. The apostles and prophets as offices belong to Volume 4. The order of the assembly in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 belongs to Pauline Doctrine Series Volume 4, and the four scenes in which the Spirit comes in Acts to Acts Commentary Volume 2. Study 86 reads Isaiah 53 as the servant passage and Study 25 reads Mark 16. This volume takes only the healing clauses of those passages, and it cites the neighbours where they have already read the ground."),
("p", "One boundary runs through the volume. The healings in Acts 9 to 28, those of Paul among them, are overlap phenomena. The volume reports them as the text reports them, does not assign them to Israel’s program and does not convert them into the Mystery. What the volume argues is what the passages say about the purpose of healing and what the record in Acts says about the end of the program the healings served. It makes no statement about God’s power."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Healing in Scripture is given to a hearer for a purpose the passage names. In the Gospels and in Acts 1 to 8 it confirms a messenger to Israel, and James 5:14 to 16 is written to the twelve tribes and belongs to Israel’s program. The gifts of healing of 1 Corinthians 12 belong to the Corinthian overlap and end with the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28. With that suspension, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. In the letters addressed to the Body, sickness is met with prayer, with ordinary care and with God’s mercy, and the pattern the letters give is the grace that is sufficient. Isaiah 53:4, 5, Matthew 8:17 and 1 Peter 2:24 stand in three different settings and are not merged into a healing in the atonement. The Body may pray for the sick; whether anyone recovers is God’s mercy, never a continuation of the Kingdom signs."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in six statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("Healing confirms a messenger", "In the Gospels and Acts 1–8 healing is given to Israel and confirms the one who speaks", "Chapters 3–5"),
    ("The gifts of healing", "The Corinthian clause belongs to the overlap and the sign-program", "Chapters 6, 7"),
    ("James 5", "Written to the twelve tribes; Israel’s passage, not the Body’s", "Chapter 8"),
    ("The letters to the Body", "Sickness is met with prayer, care and mercy; no healing ministry is given", "Chapters 9, 10"),
    ("Isaiah 53, Matthew 8, 1 Peter 2", "Three settings, kept apart; none promises healing to the Body", "Chapters 4, 11"),
    ("The end", "Healing ends programmatically at Acts 28; no verse states it", "Chapter 12"),
  ], [0.26, 0.5, 0.24]),
]
